# CinemaScanner project context

## Goal

CinemaScanner helps users find movie screenings in Israel.

Planned flow:

1. User enters a location (or uses current location) and a date.
2. The backend finds cinemas within a configurable radius.
3. It fetches screenings from the relevant cinema chains.
4. The frontend displays movie, time, cinema, distance, and movie metadata.
5. Only when the user opens a screening do we fetch the seat map/status.
6. The app calculates occupancy and recommends available central seat pairs.

## Cinema research

The following chains have been investigated:

- Cinema City: schedule, booking links, seat layout, and seat status are available.
- Yes Planet: schedule, booking links, `seatplanV2`, and `seats-statusV2` are available.
- Hot Cinema: schedule and booking links work. Seat maps render through the Bigger Picture platform; a session token is required for the seat API.
- Lev Cinema: schedule and booking links work. The booking page renders an interactive PresGlobal seat map with available, occupied, selected, and accessible seats.
- Movieland: schedule and booking links work. The booking page renders a Bigger Picture seat map; the seat API requires a session token.

Important endpoint patterns found during research:

### Cinema City

```text
GET https://www.cinema-city.co.il/tickets/Movies
GET https://www.cinema-city.co.il/tickets/Events?MovieId={movie_id}&Date={dd/mm/yyyy}
```

Booking URL pattern:

```text
https://www.cinema-city.co.il/order/?eventID={event_id}&theaterId={theater_id}
```

Seat endpoints:

```text
https://tickets.cinema-city.co.il/api/seats/seatplanV2?venueId={venue_id}&seatplanId={seatplan_id}
https://tickets.cinema-city.co.il/api/seats/seats-statusV2?presentationId={presentation_id}&venueTypeId=1&isReserved=1
```

The status keys use the form `section_internalSeatId_internalRowId`. The layout
maps internal IDs to displayed row/seat numbers and physical coordinates.

### Bigger Picture (Hot Cinema and Movieland)

The public API uses endpoints similar to:

```text
/cus/event/{event_id}/seatsStatus
/cus/seatStatus/venue/{venue}/venueSection/{section}/event/{event}
/cus/venue/{venue}/venueSeatPlan/{seat_plan_id}/full
```

Unauthenticated requests return `401 No Session token found`. A browser booking
session creates a temporary bearer token. Do not store tokens permanently or
commit them to the repository.

## Technology decisions

- Backend: Python + FastAPI
- Frontend: React + TypeScript (planned)
- Database: PostgreSQL later; not required for the first live-data MVP
- Browser/session automation: Playwright if needed for session-based providers
- Project style: modular monolith, not microservices

## Current backend

The backend is under `backend/` and currently contains:

- FastAPI app in `backend/app/main.py`
- Pydantic models for cinemas, movies, screenings, and seats
- Static cinema configuration in `backend/app/data/cinemas.py`
- Adapter protocol in `backend/app/adapters/base.py`
- Adapter registry in `backend/app/adapters/registry.py`
- Routes for health, cinemas, screenings, and placeholder seat lookup
- Tests in `backend/tests/`

Current API routes:

```text
GET /health
GET /api/cinemas
GET /api/cinemas?chain=cinema_city
GET /api/screenings?date=2026-09-08
GET /api/screenings/{screening_id}/seats
```

The screening route has registered live Cinema City and Yes Planet adapters.
Configured live branches are `cinema-city-glilot` (provider ID 1170) and
`yes-planet-ayalon` (provider ID 1025). The former Cinema City Ayalon placeholder
was replaced by Glilot because it did not map to a real provider branch.
The seat route intentionally returns 501. Schedule failures return 502;
unknown filters and unimplemented chains return an empty list.

Planet schedules use:
`https://www.planetcinema.co.il/il/data-api-service/v1/quickbook/10100/film-events/in-cinema/{cinema_id}/at-date/{YYYY-MM-DD}`.
The adapter joins `body.events` to `body.films`, reads the requested and previous
business days, and filters by Israel calendar date. Booking links and movie
metadata come from the response. Cinema-specific parsing stays inside adapters.

All automated tests use mocked HTTP; a test guard blocks real provider requests.
Live checks on 2026-09-13 returned 68 Planet Ayalon and 97 Cinema City Glilot
screenings. Counts change over time; use a current date in manual checks.

## Development conventions

- Keep cinema-specific parsing inside adapters.
- Normalize every provider into the shared `Screening` and `SeatMap` models.
- Do not fabricate data when an endpoint fails or a field is unknown.
- Keep schedule fetching read-only; do not select, hold, or purchase seats.
- Treat provider endpoints as undocumented and subject to change.
- Do not commit cookies, bearer tokens, API keys, or other secrets.

## Completed initial schedule task

Implement the Cinema City schedule adapter:

1. Fetch `/tickets/Movies`.
2. Fetch `/tickets/Events` for each movie and requested date.
3. Filter events to the requested cinema.
4. Convert them to the normalized `Screening` model.
5. Register the adapter in `app/adapters/registry.py`.
6. Make `GET /api/screenings?date=YYYY-MM-DD` return real Cinema City results.
7. Add tests using mocked HTTP responses; tests must not depend on live cinema sites.
